    "text": "The contradiction arises from differing scopes: A frames memory-to-plan translation as a sub-problem of partial observability for architectures with long-term memory; B defines the process without specifying its status as an independent challenge.",
    "topic": "Scope ambiguity"
  },
  {
    "text": "In WorldLines, memory-to-plan translation is introduced to jointly handle partial observability and overwritten world states, not as a standalone fundamental problem.",
    "topic": "Evidence from existing literature"
  },
  {
    "text": "A challenge is 'fundamental' if it is necessary for all embodied agents; translating long-term memory into plans is only necessary if the agent's architecture includes a long-term memory module.",
    "topic": "Definition of fundamental challenge"
  },
  {
    "text": "The disagreement can be resolved by clarifying that B’s description is a neutral account of the conversion process, while A’s claim concerns its status within a taxonomy of challenges.",
    "topic": "Resolution proposal"
  }
]
